::ALG2_U03_L05_GP01::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L05_GP01
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Simplify 3/x + 4/x.
{
= 7/x, x != 0#Correct. The denominators match, so add numerators and keep x != 0.
~ 7/(2x)#Matching denominators are not added.
~ 12/x, x != 0#The numerators add, not multiply.
~ 7/x, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_GP02::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L05_GP02
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Simplify 1/x + 2/(x + 1).
{
~ 3/(2x + 1)#Denominators are not added.
= (3x + 1)/(x(x + 1)), x != 0 and x != -1#Correct. Use LCD x(x + 1), then combine x + 1 + 2x.
~ (x + 3)/(x(x + 1)), x != 0 and x != -1#The numerator should be x + 1 + 2x.
~ (3x + 1)/(x + 1), x != -1#The full LCD remains in the denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_GP03::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L05_GP03
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Simplify 5/(x - 2) - 1/(x - 2).
{
~ 4/(2x - 4)#Matching denominators stay the same.
~ 6/(x - 2), x != 2#The operation is subtraction, not addition.
= 4/(x - 2), x != 2#Correct. The denominators match, so subtract numerators and keep the restriction.
~ 4/(x - 2), no restriction#The denominator excludes x = 2.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_GP04::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L05_GP04
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the LCD of 1/x and 1/(x + 3)?
{
~ 2x + 3#Denominators are not added.
~ x + 3#This misses the factor x.
~ x#This misses the factor x + 3.
= x(x + 3)#Correct. Both denominator factors are needed in the least common denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L05_GP05::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L05_GP05
MLA Standard: Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Simplify 2/(x + 4) + 3/(x + 4).
{
= 5/(x + 4), x != -4#Correct. Add numerators over the common denominator and keep x != -4.
~ 5/(2x + 8)#The common denominator is not added to itself.
~ 6/(x + 4), x != -4#The numerators add to 5, not 6.
~ 5/(x + 4), no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = -4.
}

